        <div class="docs-content">
            <section id="overview">
                <h2>Overview</h2>
                <p>Query Gate is a .NET 10 Blazor Server application for governed database access across SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL and MariaDB. Users work in a query workspace without seeing database credentials. Permissions, approvals and audit records control and document every execution.</p>
                <p>Key capabilities: permission-gated SQL, multi-engine support (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB), time-boxed access grants, audit-first execution, passkey / TOTP authentication, an access-request workflow, and CSV export with per-user export permissions.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="user-guide">
                <h2>User guide</h2>
                <p>Query Gate provides engineers and auditors with controlled database access while eliminating credential sprawl.</p>
                <div class="docs-guide-cards">
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="terminal" /> Query workspace</h3>
                        <p>Select your database (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB), write SQL with Monaco syntax assistance and keyboard shortcuts (<kbd>F5</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl+Enter</kbd>), and execute queries. Results appear in a responsive data grid.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="shield" /> SQL Gate validation</h3>
                        <p>Queries are parsed and verified using Microsoft ScriptDom before execution. Disallowed statements, cross-database queries, or forbidden system procedures are denied upfront.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="key" /> Access requests &amp; approval</h3>
                        <p>Need temporary or elevated access? Request access directly from the Access page. Administrators review, approve with expiration dates, or reject requests with full audit records.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="download" /> Auditing &amp; CSV export</h3>
                        <p>Every executed statement is recorded with execution duration, outcome, and user identity. Users with <code>EXPORT:CSV</code> permission can download query results directly.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
            <section id="iis">
                <h2>Deploy to IIS on Windows</h2>
                <p>Install IIS, the WebSocket Protocol feature and the .NET 10 Hosting Bundle. Publish the project:</p>
                <pre><code>dotnet publish DbAccessManager.csproj -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained false -o ./publish</code></pre>
                <p>Deploy the published files, including <code>web.config</code>, to an IIS site at the domain root. Configure a dedicated application pool with No Managed Code, bind a trusted HTTPS certificate, and give the pool identity access to a persistent data protection key directory. Set the application configuration and run the first migration with one instance, as described in the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate/blob/main/docs/DEPLOYMENT.md#iis-on-windows">IIS guide</a>.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="azure">
                <h2>Deploy to Azure App Service</h2>
                <p>Create a Windows App Service with a .NET 10 runtime and connectivity to your SQL Server. Enable HTTPS Only and WebSockets, and use one instance. Publish the application and deploy the contents of the publish directory.</p>
                <p>Set the SQL connection, encryption and JWT keys, bootstrap credentials and a persistent data protection key path in App Service configuration. Apply migrations on the first start, then remove bootstrap credentials. See the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate/blob/main/docs/DEPLOYMENT.md#azure-app-service-windows">Azure guide</a> for settings and upgrade steps.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="linux">
                <h2>Deploy to a Linux server</h2>
                <p>Install the .NET 10 ASP.NET Core runtime, publish the application, and run it under a dedicated service account with systemd. The example uses Kestrel HTTPS directly with a trusted PFX certificate and a persistent key directory.</p>
                <p>Configure SQL Server access, secrets and migrations before starting the service. See the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate/blob/main/docs/DEPLOYMENT.md#linux-server-with-systemd">Linux guide</a> for a systemd unit and environment settings.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="docker">
                <h2>Deploy with Docker</h2>
                <p>Run Query Gate with Docker Compose and an existing, reachable SQL Server. You need Docker with Compose, a SQL Server application database, and a password-protected HTTPS PFX certificate trusted by your browser and matching the host name.</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Clone the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate">GitHub repository</a> and copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code>.</li>
                    <li>Set the SQL connection string, bootstrap administrator credentials, encryption and JWT keys, and certificate path and password in <code>.env</code>. Generate keys with <code>openssl rand -base64 32</code> (encryption) and <code>openssl rand -base64 64</code> (JWT).</li>
                    <li>Set <code>APPLY_MIGRATIONS=true</code> for the first start. The SQL account needs schema migration permissions. Start a single instance:</li>
                </ol>
                <pre><code>docker compose up -d --build
docker compose logs -f query-gate</code></pre>
                <ol start="4">
                    <li>Open <code>https://localhost:8443</code> (or the host name on your certificate) and confirm migrations succeed.</li>
                    <li>Set <code>APPLY_MIGRATIONS=false</code> and restart: <code>docker compose up -d</code>.</li>
                    <li>Remove the bootstrap credentials from <code>.env</code> after the administrator account has been created.</li>
                </ol>
                <p>A connection string inside a container must use a SQL Server address reachable from the container. <code>localhost</code> refers to the container itself. On Docker Desktop, <code>host.docker.internal</code> can reach a SQL Server on the host.</p>
                <p>For detailed setup, upgrades, backups and security notes, see the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate/blob/main/docs/DOCKER.md">Docker deployment guide</a>.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="configuration">
                <h2>Configuration and operations</h2>
                <p>All secrets and certificates must be kept private and backed up securely. Losing the encryption key makes stored database connections unreadable.</p>
                <table class="docs-table">
                    <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>ConnectionStrings__Default</code></td><td>SQL Server connection string for the Query Gate application database.</td><td><code>Server=.;Database=QueryGate;...</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Encryption__Key</code></td><td>Base-64 key used to encrypt stored database connection strings. <strong>Back this up.</strong></td><td><code>openssl rand -base64 32</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Jwt__Key</code></td><td>Base-64 key used to sign session tokens. Keep consistent across restarts.</td><td><code>openssl rand -base64 64</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Bootstrap__AdminEmail</code></td><td>Email for the initial administrator account. Remove after first sign-in.</td><td><code>admin@example.com</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Bootstrap__AdminPassword</code></td><td>Password for the initial administrator account. Remove after first sign-in.</td><td>Min 12 chars, mixed case + digit</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>APPLY_MIGRATIONS</code></td><td>Set <code>true</code> to run EF migrations on startup (Docker only). Set <code>false</code> after first run.</td><td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ASPNETCORE_Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Path</code></td><td>Path to the HTTPS PFX certificate file.</td><td><code>/certs/cert.pfx</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ASPNETCORE_Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Password</code></td><td>Password for the PFX certificate.</td><td>—</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Keep the same JWT key across restarts. Apply migrations with one instance at a time after a database backup. Data protection keys must be stored on a persistent volume and protected at rest.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="first-steps">
                <h2>First steps</h2>
                <p>Once deployed, the bootstrap administrator account is created from the <code>Bootstrap__AdminEmail</code> and <code>Bootstrap__AdminPassword</code> environment variables on first start.</p>
                <ol>
                    <li><a href="/Account/Login">Sign in</a> as the bootstrap administrator.</li>
                    <li>Register a target database (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, or MariaDB) using a dedicated, least-privilege account.</li>
                    <li>Create users and grant permissions or review their access requests.</li>
                    <li>Open the query workspace to run permitted SQL and review activity in the audit log.</li>
                    <li>Remove the bootstrap credentials from your environment after setup is complete.</li>
                </ol>
            </section>
            <section id="troubleshooting">
                <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
                <dl class="docs-dl">
                    <dt>Migrations fail on first start</dt>
                    <dd>The SQL account must have <code>db_owner</code> or schema-create permissions on the application database during migration. Use a separate migration account and downgrade it afterwards.</dd>

                    <dt>Certificate errors in the browser</dt>
                    <dd>The PFX certificate must be trusted by the browser and the subject / SAN must match the host name exactly. Self-signed certificates must be added to the OS trust store. On Docker Desktop, use <code>host.docker.internal</code> in both the cert SAN and the browser URL.</dd>

                    <dt>"localhost" cannot reach SQL Server in Docker</dt>
                    <dd>Inside a container, <code>localhost</code> resolves to the container itself. Use <code>host.docker.internal</code> (Docker Desktop) or the host's LAN IP to reach a SQL Server running on the host machine.</dd>

                    <dt>Data protection key errors after restart</dt>
                    <dd>The data protection key directory must be on a persistent volume. If the keys are lost, existing authentication cookies and encrypted values become invalid. Mount the key directory as a named volume in your Compose file.</dd>

                    <dt>Users see "Account inactive or expired"</dt>
                    <dd>An administrator must activate the account and optionally set an expiry date. Check the Users admin page and ensure the account is active.</dd>

                    <dt>Five failed login attempts — account locked</dt>
                    <dd>Accounts lock for 15 minutes after five consecutive failed password attempts. An administrator can unlock the account early from the Users admin page.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>
